content.text:
This is the Hash Code 2015 "Optimize a Data Center" problem. The program has two phases: place servers in rows, then assign them to pools.

**Placement.** Sort servers by capacity/size ratio, descending. For each server in that order, put it in the row with the lowest current total capacity (to balance rows) that has a contiguous free segment large enough. Within a row, use the first-fit segment.

Each row is a list of free segments between unavailable slots. Row choice tries rows in order of increasing capacity, up to R rows, and stops at the first that fits. A fit check is O(segments), so the total cost is about M·R·segments in the worst case. With M up to 1e6 and R up to 1000 this could be slow, but rows that fail to fit are rare early. To bound the cost, I keep each row's maximum free segment length and skip any row where that maximum is less than the server size. The row choice then costs O(R) per server and the segment scan is done once. This gives 1e6 × 1000 = 1e9 simple operations in the worst case, which is too heavy. Instead, I use a priority-queue-like approach: a std::set of (capacity, row) pairs, iterated from the lowest. Rows that cannot fit are skipped, and a row that fails for a size is rarely needed again. Smaller sizes may still fit later, so I do not remove rows permanently. To bound the work, I cap the number of rows tried per server at 50 and fall back to a full scan only if none of those fit. The full scan is rare because it only happens once space is nearly exhausted.

**Pool assignment.** Process the placed servers in descending capacity order. For each, choose the pool that minimizes its guaranteed capacity, using a greedy rule: give the server to the pool with the lowest guaranteed capacity, breaking ties toward the pool with the lowest capacity in the server's row. For each pool I keep a per-row capacity array and a total. Computing the guaranteed capacity is total minus the max row value. P × R is up to 1e6 entries, which is fine. The max row value is maintained incrementally, since values only increase. The pool is picked with a set keyed by (gc, pool). After the assignment I update that pool's entry.

A server is added to a pool by increasing its row value and total, then recomputing gc = total − maxrow. Simply picking the lowest-gc pool can put a server in the row that is already that pool's max, which wastes it. I accept this heuristic. To improve it, I examine the lowest-gc pools in order, up to a few candidates, and choose the one where adding the server to its row raises gc the most. The candidate scan is limited to 8 pools via set iteration.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;
int main(){
    int R,S,U,P,M;
    scanf("%d %d %d %d %d",&R,&S,&U,&P,&M);
    vector<vector<char>> bad(R,vector<char>(S,0));
    for(int i=0;i<U;i++){int r,s;scanf("%d %d",&r,&s);bad[r][s]=1;}
    vector<int> z(M),c(M);
    for(int i=0;i<M;i++)scanf("%d %d",&z[i],&c[i]);
    // segments per row: start,len
    vector<vector<pair<int,int>>> seg(R);
    for(int r=0;r<R;r++){
        int s=0;
        while(s<S){
            if(bad[r][s]){s++;continue;}
            int e=s;
            while(e<S&&!bad[r][e])e++;
            seg[r].push_back({s,e-s});
            s=e;
        }
    }
    vector<int> ord(M);
    iota(ord.begin(),ord.end(),0);
    stable_sort(ord.begin(),ord.end(),[&](int a,int b){
        return (long long)c[a]*z[b]>(long long)c[b]*z[a];
    });
    vector<long long> rowcap(R,0);
    set<pair<long long,int>> rs;
    for(int r=0;r<R;r++)rs.insert({0,r});
    vector<int> ar(M,-1),as(M,-1),ap(M,-1);
    auto tryRow=[&](int r,int i)->bool{
        for(auto&sg:seg[r]){
            if(sg.second>=z[i]){
                as[i]=sg.first;ar[i]=r;
                sg.first+=z[i];sg.second-=z[i];
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    };
    for(int i:ord){
        int placed=-1;
        int tried=0;
        for(auto it=rs.begin();it!=rs.end()&&tried<50;++it,++tried){
            if(tryRow(it->second,i)){placed=it->second;break;}
        }
        if(placed<0){
            int k=0;
            for(auto it=rs.begin();it!=rs.end();++it,++k){
                if(k<50)continue;
                if(tryRow(it->second,i)){placed=it->second;break;}
            }
        }
        if(placed>=0){
            rs.erase({rowcap[placed],placed});
            rowcap[placed]+=c[i];
            rs.insert({rowcap[placed],placed});
        }
    }
    // pools
    vector<vector<long long>> pr(P,vector<long long>(R,0));
    vector<long long> tot(P,0),mx(P,0);
    set<pair<long long,int>> ps;
    for(int p=0;p<P;p++)ps.insert({0,p});
    for(int i:ord){
        if(ar[i]<0)continue;
        int r=ar[i];
        int bestp=-1;long long bestg=-1;
        int k=0;
        for(auto it=ps.begin();it!=ps.end()&&k<8;++it,++k){
            int p=it->second;
            long long nm=max(mx[p],pr[p][r]+c[i]);
            long long g=tot[p]+c[i]-nm;
            if(g>bestg){bestg=g;bestp=p;}
        }
        int p=bestp;
        ps.erase({tot[p]-mx[p],p});
        pr[p][r]+=c[i];
        tot[p]+=c[i];
        mx[p]=max(mx[p],pr[p][r]);
        ps.insert({tot[p]-mx[p],p});
        ap[i]=p;
    }
    for(int i=0;i<M;i++){
        if(ar[i]<0)puts("x");
        else printf("%d %d %d\n",ar[i],as[i],ap[i]);
    }
}
```
